Below is a self-contained MATLAB script/function that converts an ultrasound time-domain voltage signal (Voltage vs time) into a single-sided magnitude spectrum in dB versus frequency in MHz. It handles cases where you provide either a time vector or a sampling frequency, applies optional windowing and zero-padding (for improved frequency resolution), and plots the result.

Copy and paste into a file (e.g., ultrasound_spectrum.m) and call it as shown in the example.

Code:
```matlab
function [f_MHz, magdB] = ultrasound_spectrum(v, t_or_Fs, varargin)
% ultrasound_spectrum Convert ultrasound voltage vs time to magnitude(dB) vs frequency(MHz)
%
% Usage:
%   [f_MHz, magdB] = ultrasound_spectrum(v, Fs)
%   [f_MHz, magdB] = ultrasound_spectrum(v, t)
%   ultrasound_spectrum(...,'Window',windowType,'Nfft',Nfft,'Plot',true)
%
% Inputs:
%   v         - voltage signal vector (time domain)
%   t_or_Fs   - either:
%                  scalar Fs (sampling frequency in Hz)
%                  vector t (time in seconds) same length as v
% Optional name-value pairs:
%   'Window'  - window type: 'none' (default), 'hamming', 'hann', 'blackman'
%   'Nfft'    - FFT length (default: nextpow2(length(v)) -> 2^nextpow2)
%   'Plot'    - true/false to produce a plot (default true)
%
% Outputs:
%   f_MHz     - frequency axis for single-sided spectrum in MHz
%   magdB     - magnitude in dB (20*log10) of single-sided spectrum

% Parse inputs
p = inputParser;
addRequired(p,'v',@(x)isvector(x) && isnumeric(x));
addRequired(p,'t_or_Fs',@(x)(isscalar(x) && isnumeric(x)) || (isvector(x) && isnumeric(x)));
addParameter(p,'Window','hamming',@ischar);
addParameter(p,'Nfft',[],@(x)isempty(x) || (isscalar(x) && x==floor(x) && x>0));
addParameter(p,'Plot',true,@islogical);
parse(p,v,t_or_Fs,varargin{:});

v = p.Results.v(:);
t_or_Fs = p.Results.t_or_Fs;
winType = lower(p.Results.Window);
Nfft = p.Results.Nfft;
doPlot = p.Results.Plot;

% Determine sampling frequency Fs
if isscalar(t_or_Fs)
    Fs = t_or_Fs;
else
    t = t_or_Fs(:);
    if length(t) ~= length(v)
        error('Time vector must be same length as signal vector.');
    end
    dt = mean(diff(t)); % assumes approximately uniform sampling
    Fs = 1/dt;
end

N = length(v);
if isempty(Nfft)
    Nfft = 2^nextpow2(N);
end

% Choose and apply window
switch winType
    case 'none'
        w = ones(N,1);
    case 'hamming'
        w = hamming(N);
    case 'hann'
        w = hann(N);
    case 'blackman'
        w = blackman(N);
    otherwise
        error('Unknown window type.');
end

% Remove DC if desired (optional). Keeping as-is but you may choose to detrend:
% v = v - mean(v);

% Apply window and zero-pad to Nfft
vwin = v .* w;
V = fft(vwin, Nfft);

% Single-sided spectrum magnitude, correct for window amplitude loss and FFT scaling
% Scale factor: 1/|sum(window)/N| to normalize amplitude to un-windowed RMS if desired.
% For typical magnitude spectrum we use: magnitude = (2/sum(w)) * abs(V(1:Nfft/2+1))
% Here use 2/N * abs(V(1:...)) adjusted for window coherent gain.
coherent_gain = sum(w)/N;              % average window value
mag_single = abs(V(1:floor(Nfft/2)+1)) * (2 / N) / coherent_gain;

% Convert to dBFS or dBV (depending on input). Use small epsilon to avoid log of zero.
epsval = 1e-20;
magdB = 20*log10(mag_single + epsval);

% Frequency axis in Hz then convert to MHz
f = (0:floor(Nfft/2))' * (Fs / Nfft);
f_MHz = f / 1e6;

% Optional plot
if doPlot
    figure;
    plot(f_MHz, magdB, 'b-','LineWidth',1.2);
    grid on;
    xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
    ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
    title('Single-sided Spectrum (Magnitude in dB)');
    xlim([0 max(f_MHz)]);
end

end
```

Example usage:
1) If you have time vector t (in seconds) and voltage v:
- [fMHz, magdB] = ultrasound_spectrum(v, t, 'Window', 'hamming', 'Nfft', 2^14);

2) If you know sampling frequency Fs (in Hz):
- [fMHz, magdB] = ultrasound_spectrum(v, Fs, 'Window', 'hann', 'Plot', true);

Notes and tips:
- For ultrasound signals, ensure Fs is sufficiently high (e.g., many MHz) to cover the frequency content.
- Nfft controls frequency resolution: larger Nfft gives finer frequency spacing but increases computation.
- Windowing reduces spectral leakage but decreases amplitude; the code compensates for coherent gain of the window.
- If you want magnitude in linear units rather than dB, return mag_single directly.
- If you'd rather have dB relative to the maximum, subtract max(magdB) to set peak to 0 dB.

If you want, I can tailor the code for a specific dataset (showing how to load a .mat or .wav file) or include a peak-finding routine to report center frequency and bandwidth.

